Z||000009||Phil||06-28-2001||07:39 PM||||Seeing that you have an 87, you should have the DIS system( no distributor) so the timing is non user adjustable. Misfiring upon accelleration is usually a sign of one or both of your coil packs going south.||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||06-28-2001||08:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR>]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What he said. BUT!!<BR>Did you check all 4 plugs?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000011||theogre||06-28-2001||08:16 PM||||Actually, backfiring when you punch it is more likely a sign of a MAP sensor problem. MAP is used to calc timing. If the MAP vac line is on the wrong port, blocked, leaking, or if the sensor itself is batty.... you get this kind of problem. The sensor doesn't have to be dead to be a problem. And yes, I've had this happen with brand new sensors. I'll only use Borg Warner or AC delco for this one now.<P>Also check MAT to the right of the TBI in the manifold. That sensor will also cause bizar problems.<P>No,,, the ECM often will not set errors because of the above sensors being marginal.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mbramble||06-29-2001||07:21 AM||||Sounds <I>very</I> much like the symptoms I had when one of my coil packs went south....||||reg||1
Z||000013||I wish I had a GT||06-30-2001||12:55 AM||||coil was one of my thoughts as well.. however.. if a coil went bad wouldn't more than one plug wire not give spark?  My thought was that one coil for 2 plugs, therefore with a bad coil to plugs wouldn't fire.... off base? or correct?||||reg||5
Z||000014||theogre||06-30-2001||01:36 PM||||A bad coil is going to kill plugs in pairs normally. You'd have to have a major insulation breakdown to only kill one plug. You'd likey fry the PIM if this happens.<P>A bad wire can kill one plug. depends just where it's bad. If the spark can get to the block then the other plug will probably fire but be weak.<P>Keep in mind. Fiero HEI and DIS use the MAP in place of having a vacuum advance. (There are some HEI with vac advance. Mainly for old non EFI V8's.)<P>When you punch the gas, the vac changes real fast. If you have a laggy MAP then you have laggy data going to the ECM and the EFI output won't match the current conditions. This can make it stall or back fire when it's punched. (I had a new, but laggy, MAP sensor that did just this. Put in a new Borg Warner and it stopped instantly.) The problem described at the top is essentially the same thing that happens with a bad vac advance on a non electronic engine.<P>Also, If the thing has backfired thru the intake then the MAT sensor has been cooked. That will need to be replaced or you can have other weird problems show up. I recomend only Borg Warner or AC delco for these as well. (Some of the off brands don't use the correct "thermistor" type and can make problems.)<P>Now if it is waiting untill the engine is hot to crap out,,,, then the shield that should live between the coils and the PIM is probably missing. That shield keeps the coils cooler and prevents EMI from the coils screwing up the PIM.<P>You can test the coils farly easy... go to any parts store and get a spark tester. connect the clamp to one coil post. connect the tip with a plug wire to the other post.<P>Set the tester for a good size gap. do this with both coils. you should see the same spark from each. Do it with the engine hot and cold... some problems are heat dependant.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fiero_24||07-18-2001||10:27 AM||||I just had the same problem with my 87 GT and it turned out to be the Ignition Module.  Autozone tested my old one for free, but if it is a heat related problem the test will say it is fine (since the part is out of the car you don't have engine heat to mess it up).  You may have replaced or tried this, but I am just passing along the info to try and help.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||4
Z||000016||84Bill||07-18-2001||01:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_24:<BR><B>I just had the same problem with my 87 GT and it turned out to be the Ignition Module.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>87 and 88 four cylinder engines have DIS (Distrubuterless Ignition Systems) and don't have Ignition Modules<BR>||||reg||12
